","NO","iso","",""],["Norway","NO","iso","",""]]},"RQTHL":{"d":[["Distribution. The species is recorded from South of Brazil (MfN) and Montevideo, in the humid subtropical coast of Uruguay (NHM – UK).","","text","",""]]},"RRN2G":{"d":[["Southern Asia","","text","",""]]},"RYDPL":{"d":[["Curaçao","","text","",""]]},"S5757":{"d":[["Distribution (Fig. 41). Based on the examined material and literature, the range of this subspecies occupies Japan, North and South Korea, East and Southwest China (Jiangsu, Jiangxi, Zhejiang, Fujian, Guangxi, Sichuan, Yunnan (except for the westernmost part), Guangdong, Hong Kong, Macao, Taiwan), Philippines, Borneo, Moluccas (Buru, Amboine, and Ternate), and Western New Guinea. The following records are based on the literature data without examination of specimens: Zhejiang (as P. f. var. thunbergi: Schauberger 1933), Guangxi (as P. flavilabris: Hua 2002), Guangdong (as P. flavilabris: Hua 2002), Macao (as P. f. var. thunbergi: Andrewes & Scott 1924), and Buru and Western New Guinea (Darlington 1968). In Japan, it is recorded from Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu and Ryukyus (Habu 1973). There is a rather wide intergradation zone between P. f. laticeps and P. f. thunbergi in Northern Vietnam and Hainan, inhabited by populations with intermediate characteristics of these two subspecies. In my opinion, the same form is also found on the Paracel Islands, South China Sea (Lemaire 2017).","","text","",""]]},"S6C9F":{"d":[["Distribution: BRAZIL — Paraná: Rio Negro; Santa Catarina: Corupá and S ã o Bento do Sul; S ã o Paulo: Engenheiro Coelho.","","text","",""]]},"S7GMM":{"d":[["Europe & Northern Asia (excluding China)","","text","",""]]},"SXPW9":{"d":[["Sweden","SE","iso","native",""]]},"T3QS":{"d":[["C-Namibia (Kalahari); Botswana; South Africa","","text","",""]]},"TGKQ4":{"d":[["Distribution. Sulawesi. Comparison. Larva of the new species T. (D.) rasus sp. n. differs from all other species of Teloganodes s. l. by very short stout setae in all setal rows of femora and tibiae (Figs 75 – 82). Male imago of T. (D.) rasus sp. n. is similar to the sympatric species T. (D.) celebensis in wings and legs coloration, shape of hind wing and shape of penis; differs by coloration of abdomen, which in T. (D.) rasus sp. n. has terga III – VI lighter than terga VII – IX (Fig. 86), in contrast to uniformly dark terga I – X in T. (D.) celebensis (Fig. 32).","","text","",""]]},"TJCJ":{"d":[["","BZL","tdwg","",""]]},"TKS5D":{"d":[["North America","","text","",""]]},"TQTR":{"d":[["COD","CD","iso","",""]]},"TWJC3":{"d":[["Denmark","DK","iso","","not evaluated"]]},"V2HNM":{"d":[["Ecuador","","text","",""]]},"V3PZX":{"d":[["New Zealand (South Isl., Stewart Isl.); Kermadec Isl.; Chatham Isl.","","text","",""]]},"VBNQT":{"d":[["Sumatra","","text","",""]]},"VCDCK":{"d":[["Gulf of California","4314","mrgid","native",""]]},"VL9P":{"d":[["Chile (Valparaíso)","","text","",""]]},"VP23":{"d":[["Australian Exclusive Economic Zone","8323","mrgid","",""],["Central and Southern Great Barrier Reef","21904","mrgid","",""],["Sulawesi Sea/Makassar Strait","22023","mrgid","",""],["Torres Strait Northern Great Barrier Reef","21905","mrgid","",""],["Torres Strait Northern Great Barrier Reef","21905","mrgid","",""]]},"WLV2":{"d":[["New Guinea","","text","",""]]},"YNKY":{"d":[["","PAN","tdwg","",""]]},"YNL8":{"d":[["","GUA","tdwg","",""]]},"Z94D":{"d":[["Kenya","","text","",""]]},"ZH63":{"d":[["","CA","iso","native",""]]}}